[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     `panic = "abort"`), plus a panic message on stderr:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     `failed printing to stdout: No space left on device (os error 28)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Cause.** `main.rs` implemented `printLine`/`printIntLine` with `println!`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `println!` unwraps the write result and panics on failure. C's `printf`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   signals errors only through its return value, which `printLine` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `printIntLine` discard, so the C program keeps going and `main` still
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `return 0`s. The final flush inside `exit` also discards its error.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Fix.** `print_line`/`print_int_line` now delegate to `cio::printf_line` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `cio::printf_int_line`, which write through `write_all` and drop the `Result`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `cio::flush()` is called before `std::process::exit(0)` and its error is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   discarded the same way. `cio.rs` already existed in the crate with exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   these semantics but was not wired into `main.rs` (the module was not even
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   declared, so it was never compiled).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Regression test.** `unwritable_stdout_does_not_change_exit_status`. Reverting
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `print_line` to `println!` makes it fail with `C Some(0) vs Rust Some(101)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No other mismatch was found. Everything below was checked and already agreed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviour that had to be preserved rather than fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places where the C does something surprising; the Rust matches it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and must keep matching it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Divide by zero in `bad()`.** `bad()` has no guard, so a `data` of `0.0F`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   makes `100.0 / data` infinite. The subsequent `(int)` cast is undefined
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   behaviour in C; the reference build compiles it to `cvttsd2si`, which yields
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the "integer indefinite" value `INT_MIN`. `cruntime::f64_to_int` returns
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `i32::MIN` for NaN and for anything outside `int` range so the printed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `-2147483648` matches. This also covers merely out-of-range quotients such as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `100.0 / 1e-30`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **NaN makes both comparisons false.** `nan` input leaves
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `fabs(data) > 0.000001` false in `goodB2G()` (so it prints the divide-by-zero
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   message) while `bad()` prints `INT_MIN`. Rust's `f64::abs` comparison and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `f64_to_int` reproduce both.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`-0`.** `fabs(-0.0) > 0.000001` is false, so `goodB2G()` reports a divide by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   zero; `bad()` computes `-inf` and still prints `-2147483648`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Reading order.** `main` calls `good()` before `bad()`, and inside `good()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `goodG2B()` reads nothing while `goodB2G()` performs the *first* `fgets`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `bad()` performs the second. So line 1 drives the `goodB2G` output and line 2
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   drives the `bad` output.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`fgets`, not `scanf`.** `CHAR_ARRAY_SIZE` is 20, so each call stores at most
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   19 bytes and stops immediately after a newline without reading past it. A line
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   of 19 or more payload bytes is therefore *split*: `goodB2G` gets the first 19
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bytes and `bad` gets the remainder (which may be just `"\n"`, i.e. `0.0`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `cruntime::fgets` reads through `Stdin`'s shared buffer so the leftover bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   survive to the next call.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`fgets` returning NULL.** Only when no byte at all was stored. With no input,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   both calls fail and `"fgets() failed."` is printed twice; with one line, only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the second fails. A read error (e.g. stdin opened on a directory) is the same
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   NULL case.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`data` stays `0.0F` when `fgets` fails**, which is why the failure path in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `bad()` still ends in the divide by zero.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`atof` never reports errors.** It is `strtod(s, NULL)` with the end pointer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   thrown away, so trailing junk is ignored (`"5abc"` is 5.0) and an input with no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   valid subject sequence is `0.0` — including `""`, `"abc"`, `"-"`, `"."`, `"e5"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and a line that begins with a NUL byte. A leading sign is discarded in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   no-conversion case, so `"-"` yields `+0.0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`atof` reads a C string.** The `fgets` buffer is interpreted only up to the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   first NUL, so `"3\0" "4"` is 3.0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Narrowing to `float`.** `data` is `float` but the division is done in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `double` (`100.0`). The `strtod` result is rounded to `f64` and then to `f32`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   before use; `atof(...) as f32` reproduces the same double rounding.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Byte-oriented input.** Neither `fgets` nor `atof` requires valid UTF-8, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the translation keeps input as `Vec<u8>` and never converts through `String`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`printLine(NULL)` is unreachable.** No caller passes NULL, but the guard is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   preserved as `Option<&str>`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`argc`/`argv` are unused**, so arguments change nothing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Output format.** `printf("%s\n", ...)` and `printf("%d\n", ...)`: no padding,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   no precision, one trailing newline each. C's stdout is fully buffered when
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   redirected and Rust's is line buffered, but since nothing is written to stderr
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the captured bytes are identical either way.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Input classes covered by `tests/differential.rs`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each branch in the C is an input class; the tests below name the branch they
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reach. All of them assert stdout, stderr and exit status.
